# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ACCIPITER FOREST FLIGHT PACKAGE
# Cooper's Hawk / Sharp-shinned Hawk biomechanics → MCO-1D
#
# BIOLOGICAL RESEARCH BASIS
# ─────────────────────────
# Cooper's hawk (Accipiter cooperii) and sharp-shinned hawk (A. striatus)
# are the most manoeuvrable medium-weight birds known. They routinely fly at
# near-full speed through dense woodland, squeezing through gaps not much
# larger than their own body width without breaking stride.
#
# Key findings from the literature (2014–2026):
#
# [1] STABILITY SWITCHING (Weston et al., J.R.Soc.Interface, March 2026)
#     The decisive finding: hawks are aerodynamically UNSTABLE in spread-wing
#     cruise (like a jet fighter — high manoeuvrability, low stability) and
#     shift to STABLE when they tuck wings for gap passage. This is the
#     opposite of conventional aircraft design. The wing tuck shifts the centre
#     of pressure aft past the centre of gravity, converting an unstable
#     configuration into a self-righting one. No active stabilisation needed
#     during the most critical phase of the passage — the bird coasts through
#     ballistically, wings tucked, in a stable glide.
#
# [2] FOUR MORPHING SHAPE MODES (Harris's hawk study, biorxiv June 2025)
#     Over 289,000 wing-tail configurations in 2,000 flights, four modes
#     capture 96% of all shape variation:
#       Mode 1: Wing LIFTING (co-occurs with tail lifting) — passive oscillation
#       Mode 2: Wing SPREADING (span extension/retraction)
#       Mode 3: Wing SWEEPING (fore/aft sweep)
#       Mode 4: Tail SPREADING (yaw/stability trim)
#     Near obstacles, subtle kinematics increase: especially wing sweep,
#     tail pitch, and hand-wing adjustments.
#
# [3] GAP NEGOTIATION KINEMATICS (Frontiers in Zoology, 2014)
#     As gap width approaches wingspan, the wingbeat cycle changes:
#       — Normal: downstroke 38ms, upstroke 22ms, ratio 0.59
#       — Near gap: upstroke EXTENDED (wings held up longer to clear the gap)
#     This is NOT stopping the wings — it is phase-managing the stroke so
#     the wings are in their minimum-span position as the body passes through.
#
# [4] SIDEWAYS APERTURE PASSAGE (hummingbird, PMC 2023)
#     For gaps smaller than one wingspan, birds use TWO strategies:
#       a. SIDEWAYS MANOEUVRE: bilateral asymmetric wing motion, body rotates
#          to present minimum cross-section, continuous flapping
#       b. BALLISTIC TUCK: wings tucked completely, no flapping, fast glide
#     The crossover between the two depends on gap size relative to wingspan.
#     Aperture > ~0.7 wingspan → sideways manoeuvre
#     Aperture < ~0.7 wingspan → ballistic tuck
#
# [5] ACCIPITER FLIGHT STYLE (Cooper's hawk specifically)
#     Short broad wings + long tail = high aspect ratio control surface aft
#     Flap-flap-GLIDE pattern (not continuous flapping like pigeons)
#     The glide phase is where gap negotiation often occurs
#     Cooper's hawk: smooth deep wingbeats, more powerful
#     Sharp-shinned: snappier, more erratic, faster reaction
#
# MCO-1D TRANSLATION
# ───────────────────
# The MCO-1D has the hardware to do all of this:
#
# STABILITY SWITCHING: The H-33/H-35 morphing system already stops rear
#     wing pairs at neutral AoA. This shifts the centre of pressure forward
#     (front wings carry all the lift). Combined with maximum aft sweep of the
#     front wings, CG-CP relationship changes. We can implement the hawk's
#     unstable-cruise → stable-tuck transition explicitly.
#
# FOUR SHAPE MODES map directly:
#     Mode 1 (wing lift):    → dihedral_deg command on all wings
#     Mode 2 (wing spread):  → already H-33 (active pair selection)
#     Mode 3 (wing sweep):   → already H-34 (sweep kinematics)
#     Mode 4 (tail spread):  → MCO-1D has no tail, but yaw phase differential
#                               acts as the equivalent
#
# GAP STROKE PHASE: The MCO-1D can hold wings at upstroke maximum (minimum
#     span position) during the passage. ODrive position control can hold
#     a specific stroke angle while the body passes through.
#
# EFFECTIVE SPAN REDUCTION:
#     Full span:        2.97m  (wings extended)
#     Swept 75° aft:   ~0.45m  (only body + tips)
#     Stopped neutral: ~0.15m  (body width only, wings back)
#     → Min flapping gap: ~0.55m  (one-wingbeat clearance each side)
#     → Min ballistic gap: ~0.15m  (body only)
#
# NEW CAPABILITIES ADDED
# ───────────────────────
# H-ACC-1  Stability switch: unstable cruise → stable tuck
# H-ACC-2  Gap stroke phase management
# H-ACC-3  Sideways aperture passage (body roll + asymmetric sweep)
# H-ACC-4  Ballistic gap tuck (wings stopped, body coasts through)
# H-ACC-5  Canopy approach profile (flap-flap-glide, decelerate to perch)
# H-ACC-6  Branch/ledge perch landing (near-hover, leg-extension analogue)
# H-ACC-7  Confined-space exit (pop-up climb from landing position)
#
# ALSS CLASS E: Forest flight curriculum (separate gated class)
# GS commands: gap_enter, gap_abort, gap_status, gap_set_width,
#              canopy_approach, perch_land, confined_exit
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

