# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DIHEDRAL STABILITY HYPOTHESES — H-DIH series
#
# Source: Fixed-wing dihedral effect research + flapping-wing stability
#         literature, August 2026
#
# THE DIHEDRAL EFFECT — HOW IT WORKS
# ─────────────────────────────────────
# Dihedral is the upward angle of the wings relative to horizontal.
# When an aircraft with dihedral enters a sideslip (say, slipping to the
# right), the right wing meets the oncoming airflow at a higher effective
# angle of attack than the left wing. This generates more lift on the
# right wing, creating a rolling moment back toward wings-level.
#
# This is NOT a direct wings-level restoring force. It is a coordination
# force: it couples roll and sideslip so that the aircraft rolls AWAY
# from the direction of the sideslip, reducing the sideslip and returning
# the aircraft to coordinated flight. Dihedral effect is coordination-seeking,
# not up-seeking.
#
# THE FULL LATERAL-DIRECTIONAL COUPLING
# ───────────────────────────────────────
# Three forces work together on a well-designed aircraft:
#
# 1. DIHEDRAL (roll-due-to-sideslip, Clβ < 0):
#    Sideslip → differential lift → roll AWAY from the slip → reduces sideslip
#    This is the passive wings-levelling tendency after a disturbance.
#
# 2. WEATHERCOCK STABILITY (yaw-due-to-sideslip, Cnβ > 0):
#    Sideslip → vertical tail side force → yaw INTO the wind → reduces sideslip
#    The aircraft aligns its nose with the relative wind, like a weathervane.
#    The MCO-1D has no vertical tail. But the CASCADE PHASE asymmetry and
#    DIHEDRAL ASYMMETRY between wing pairs create an equivalent yaw moment.
#
# 3. COORDINATED TURN (the natural consequence of 1+2 working together):
#    When the pilot banks to turn, the lowered wing generates less lift,
#    the aircraft begins to sideslip toward the low wing, dihedral raises
#    the low wing back up slightly, and weathercock yaws the nose into the
#    turn. The result is a naturally coordinated turn without rudder input —
#    exactly what a well-designed aircraft does.
#
# THE FLAPPING-WING EQUIVALENT
# ──────────────────────────────
# The fruit-fly / flapping-robot research (Muijres et al., 2022) proved:
#   - Flying insects use STROKE DIHEDRAL (asymmetry between upstroke and
#     downstroke stroke-plane angles) to create passive sideslip stability.
#   - CFD confirmed: the coupling between lateral motion and stroke dihedral
#     passively steers a flapping flyer into the direction of a sideways gust.
#   - Yaw coupling passively steers INTO the wind.
#   - Roll coupling is stabilising at higher gust velocities.
#
# The HIT-Hawk research confirmed: an equivalent geometric dihedral angle
# of 17.5° (mid-stroke position) produced "exceptional rolling stability
# and crosswind resistance during outdoor flight."
#
# For the MCO-1D, BOTH geometric dihedral AND stroke-path dihedral
# are available as independent control parameters.
#
# KEY INSIGHT FOR COORDINATED TURNS WITHOUT RUDDER
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The most powerful effect helping coordinate turns is the directional
# stability of the aircraft. Increasing weathercock stability reduces the
# amount the aircraft yaws in response to a given yawing moment. If the
# aircraft is stable enough, adverse yaw from roll inputs will not cause
# much sideslip, and turns will be nearly self-coordinating.
#
# On the MCO-1D, weathercock stability comes from:
#   - Geometric dihedral (already present, 5-7° in most configs)
#   - Stroke dihedral asymmetry (upstroke higher than downstroke)
#   - Cascade phase for zero net yaw torque (H-PREC-2)
#   - Proverse yaw mechanism (H-MV-1, already in genome)
#
# What is MISSING: hypotheses that specifically optimise these mechanisms
# TOGETHER for natural coordination, rather than targeting them individually.
#
# HYPOTHESES: H-DIH-1 through H-DIH-6
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

