# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# PROVERSE YAW + DIHEDRAL INTEGRATION — H-PDW series
#
# "PDW" = Proverse-Dihedral-Weathercock — the three mechanisms that together
# produce natural coordination and straight tracking without rudder input.
#
# THE INTEGRATION PROBLEM
# ──────────────────────────
# All three mechanisms exist and work individually. The gap is that they have
# never been optimised together as a system.
#
# THE RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN THE THREE
# ─────────────────────────────────────
# Think of it as three forces acting in a turn:
#
#   1. PROVERSE YAW (inner-wing amplitude reduction):
#      Roll command → asymmetric amplitude → drag differential → yaw INTO turn
#      Effect: reduces adverse yaw, prevents sideslip at turn entry
#      Analogy: rudder coordination applied automatically
#
#   2. DIHEDRAL (stroke plane angle):
#      Sideslip → asymmetric effective AoA → roll AWAY from sideslip
#      Effect: if sideslip develops anyway, dihedral fights it
#      Analogy: self-levelling tendency
#
#   3. WEATHERCOCK (cascade phase):
#      Sideslip → asymmetric force timing → yaw INTO wind
#      Effect: aircraft aligns nose with relative wind passively
#      Analogy: vertical tail — but implemented via cascade
#
# THE CORRECT BALANCE
# ────────────────────
# Proverse handles turn ENTRY (prevents adverse yaw as bank is established).
# Weathercock handles turn MAINTENANCE (keeps nose tracking through the turn).
# Dihedral handles DISTURBANCES (gusts pushing the wings, sideslip from
# turbulence).
#
# If proverse is too strong relative to dihedral: the aircraft overbanks in
# sustained turns (the nose yaws into the turn faster than the bank warrants).
# If dihedral is too strong relative to weathercock: Dutch roll (oscillation
# between roll and yaw, each feeding the other).
# If weathercock is too weak: the aircraft wanders in heading hands-off.
#
# The genome must learn all three simultaneously. That requires hypotheses
# that vary them TOGETHER, scored on a metric that captures how well they
# work as a system — not individually.
#
# HYPOTHESES: H-PDW-1 through H-PDW-3
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

