# WATER OPERATIONS HYPOTHESES — H-WD / H-WL series
#
# Sources:
#   Seaplane doctrine: FAA Seaplane/Skiplane/Float Helicopter Operations
#                      Handbook, Transport Canada Seaplane Rating Guide,
#                      AOPA Glassy Water technique articles
#   Seagull research:  Clap-and-fling + water surface synergy = 22% bonus
#                      Fast-up slow-down stroke Q ≈ 2.0 during departure run
#   Duck research:     Wing-foot phase synchrony (front thrust / rear lift)
#                      Ankle impulse = 3× terrestrial power at departure
#
# DESIGN PRINCIPLE — AVIATION-FIRST, NOT BIOLOGY-FIRST:
#   The duck tau-dot controlled-collision strategy was removed because
#   τ̇ = 0.90 deliberately plans for residual kinetic energy at contact,
#   which is safe for a compliant animal hitting a compliant surface but
#   NOT appropriate for an aircraft with structural limits.
#
#   The correct approach for rough water is established seaplane doctrine:
#   "Touch down just after the crest. Minimum speed. Shallow descent.
#    Constant attitude. Power-managed, not attitude-chased."
#   (FAA, Transport Canada, AOPA — all consistent on this point)
#
# ROUGH WATER SOLUTION — WHAT AVIATION SAYS:
#   1. MINIMUM SPEED — slowest possible IAS at touchdown
#      Lower speed = lower kinetic energy = lower structural loads
#      The MCO-1D minimum speed is approximately stall + margin = 5.5 m/s
#
#   2. SHALLOW CONSTANT DESCENT — 100-200 ft/min, not chased
#      Power manages the descent rate. Attitude held constant.
#      The glassy water controller already does 0.1 m/s (20 ft/min) —
#      this is correct. No change needed there.
#
#   3. JUST AFTER THE CREST — not into the face, not into the trough
#      Landing just after the crest means the hull meets the water on
#      the back face of the wave (decreasing height) rather than:
#      - Crest: maximum structural impact (wave is at maximum height)
#      - Front face: wave moving toward aircraft (worst case)
#      - Trough: deeper water, more spray, possible hull slamming
#      Just-after-crest gives minimum hull impact velocity AND the wave
#      is retreating, reducing secondary impact.
#      This is NOT a controlled collision. The aircraft is already at
#      near-stall minimum speed with shallow descent. The wave timing
#      reduces the residual contact energy further — but the primary
#      protection is minimum speed, not timing.
#
#   4. CONSTANT ATTITUDE — do not flare aggressively
#      Aggressive flare over water risks misjudging height (especially
#      on glassy or rough water) and stalling too high above the surface.
#      Hold the landing attitude and let the water come to you.
#
# WHAT STAYS FROM THE BIOLOGY:
#   The departure hypotheses (seagull clap synergy, duck phase coordination,
#   asymmetric stroke Q) are valid and retained — they address takeoff
#   performance, not landing safety. The biology informs what to do
#   when generating lift. It does not override aviation safety doctrine
#   for landing.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

