# H-BS REVISION — September 2026
#
# H-BS-2 (slope landing) and H-BS-3 (STOL departure sequence) REMOVED —
# both required the software to manage approach angle, transition heights,
# and power timing that belong to the pilot. The pilot lands uphill with
# back pressure. The pilot manages the departure with stick and throttle.
# These are pilot techniques, not background wing kinematics.
#
# H-BS-1 REWRITTEN — snow surface kinematics only. The pilot applies back
# pressure as on any soft-field takeoff. The hypothesis discovers what
# cascade and amplitude profile the wings should use on snow surfaces to
# help the aircraft overcome the initial drag spike and plane onto the
# packed layer. The pilot flies; the wings adapt invisibly.
#
# H-BS-5 EXPANDED — nap-of-earth requires quickness in all three axes,
# not just pitch. Roll authority to dodge obstacles laterally. Yaw authority
# to align with a gap or a gully. Pitch to clear terrain. All three must
# be at the same 0.8-second response standard.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

