# H-FD: PIGEON STEEP DESCENT — VARIABLE DIHEDRAL GLIDE PATH CONTROL
#
# Source: Berg & Biewener (2010) — Wing and body kinematics of takeoff
#         and landing flight in the pigeon (Columba livia)
#         J. Exp. Biol. 213:1651-1658
#
# The observation: During a steep descent, pigeons hold the body flat
# and use the wings as the primary control surface, raising them to
# high dihedral angles while tilting the stroke plane progressively
# upward. This creates a large drag surface that controls both
# descent rate and airspeed, while the flat body keeps ride quality
# smooth — critical for fragile cargo or passengers.
#
# The mechanism is NOT primarily geometric dihedral as a passive
# stabiliser (which H-DIH covers at 7-12°). It is ACTIVE HIGH-DIHEDRAL
# as a DRAG AND LIFT MODULATION TOOL at 40-70° — used intentionally
# to fly a precise glide path at controlled speed.
#
# GAP confirmed by audit: max dihedral in any current hypothesis = 12°.
# Discovery bounds allow to 20°. Pigeon uses 50-70°.
# No hypothesis uses high dihedral as a PRIMARY control mechanism.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

